#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.725pt, 804.24pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 83.28pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(96pt, 103.92pt, "As power densities continue to increase in microprocessors and artificial intelligence ")
  #line-at(72pt, 124.56pt, "hardware [1], data centers [2], and power electronics [3], thermal management is increasingly ")
  #line-at(72pt, 145.44pt, "limited not only by heat sinks and spreaders, but also by the thermal resistance along the heat-")
  #line-at(72pt, 166.08pt, "flow path from the heat-generating device to the external cooling structure [1, 3, 4]. In many ")
  #line-at(72pt, 186.72pt, "practical packages, the dominant temperature drop can occur across the thermal interface ")
  #line-at(72pt, 207.36pt, "between contacting solids rather than within the solids themselves. Thermal interface materials ")
  #line-at(72pt, 228.24pt, "(TIMs) are therefore widely introduced to fill interfacial voids created by surface roughness, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 248.88pt, "waviness, and non-planarity, thereby reducing the resistance to heat flow from the chip or ")
  #line-at(72pt, 269.52pt, "module to the heat spreader, heat sink, or cold plate [1, 5]. In this sense, TIMs are not merely ")
  #line-at(72pt, 290.16pt, "passive fillers; they are functional elements that directly regulate heat diffusion, heat storage, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 311.04pt, "and contact heat transfer in the overall thermal process of electronic assemblies. ")
  #line-at(96pt, 331.68pt, "For a bonded TIM layer, thermal performance is governed by at least three quantities: the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 352.32pt, "thermal conductivity ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(176.172pt, 352.32pt, "k")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(181.498pt, 352.32pt, " of the TIM bulk, the volumetric heat capacity ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(412.988pt, 352.32pt, "C")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(420.992pt, 352.32pt, " of the layer, and the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 372.96pt, "interfacial thermal resistance (ITR) at the contacting surfaces. The first controls heat diffusion ")
  #line-at(72pt, 393.84pt, "through the TIM, the second governs transient thermal storage within the layer, and the third ")
  #line-at(72pt, 414.48pt, "reflects the efficiency of heat transmission across imperfect contact. While steady-state cooling ")
  #line-at(72pt, 435.12pt, "analyses often emphasize only the overall thermal resistance, transient operation under pulsed ")
  #line-at(72pt, 455.76pt, "power, load fluctuations, or thermal cycling also depends strongly on the thermal capacitance ")
  #line-at(72pt, 476.64pt, "of the interface region [6]. Accordingly, meaningful evaluation of TIMs for modern packaging ")
  #line-at(72pt, 497.28pt, "requires simultaneous knowledge of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(248.625pt, 497.28pt, "k")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.951pt, 497.28pt, ", ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(259.951pt, 497.28pt, "C")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(267.955pt, 497.28pt, ", and ITR rather than any one parameter alone. ")
  #line-at(96pt, 517.92pt, "A further complication is that TIM properties are not always intrinsic constants under ")
  #line-at(72pt, 538.56pt, "practical assembly conditions. Commercial TIMs are typically compressed during installation, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 559.44pt, "and their internal structure and contact state can evolve with pressure. Previous work has shown ")
  #line-at(72pt, 580.08pt, "that the overall thermal conductance of TIM joints may increase substantially with clamping ")
  #line-at(72pt, 600.72pt, "pressure [7]. Thermal contact theory also indicates that asperity deformation and interfacial ")
  #line-at(72pt, 621.36pt, "conformity during initial compression are only partially reversible, so that the same nominal ")
  #line-at(72pt, 642.24pt, "pressure can correspond to different real contact areas during loading and unloading [8]. For ")
  #line-at(72pt, 662.88pt, "soft TIMs, such as gels, greases, and compliant pads, compression may additionally alter void ")
  #line-at(72pt, 683.52pt, "content, filler connectivity, local wetting, and bond-line morphology. These effects imply that ")
  #line-at(72pt, 704.16pt, "the thermal response of the assembled interface can be both pressure-dependent and history-")
  #line-at(72pt, 725.04pt, "dependent. Such state dependence is directly relevant to packaging design because junction-to-")
  #line-at(72pt, 745.68pt, "case or junction-to-sink thermal impedance under service conditions is controlled by the actual ")
]

